WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
AI imposes super weird constraints on your code base.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.880
And most code bases out there in the world

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.360
probably including yours are not ready.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.400
Your code base way more than the prompt

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.160
that you used way more than your agents.md file

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.800
is the biggest influence on AI's output.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.160
And if it's designed wrong,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.360
it can cost you in a bunch of different ways.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.040
It can mean that the AI doesn't receive feedback fast enough

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.800
so it doesn't know if what it changed

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.200
actually did what it intended.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.640
It can find it super hard to make sense of things

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.400
and find files and work out even how to test things.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.960
And finally, it can lead you into cognitive burnout

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.000
as you try to hold together AI and your code base

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.400
and patch it all up and keep everything in your mind.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.760
And my thesis here is that software quality matters

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.080
more than ever.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.640
In other words, how easy your code base is to change

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.240
makes a huge impact on how AI then goes and changes it.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.440
And the stuff that we've known about software best practices

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.360
for 20 years still holds more true than ever.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.920
And if you're interested in getting better at this stuff,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.240
then check out my newsletter, AI hero.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.680
I teach you all about AI coding,

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.240
but this is not for vibe coders.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.280
This is for real engineers solving real problems.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.320
And if that's you and you're not sure how to handle

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.000
these new tools, then you are gonna love it.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.160
Now, let's imagine that this here is our code base.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.800
Each one of these little squares represents a module

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.960
and this module might export some functionality,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.520
it might export a function, might export some variables,

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.760
might export a component if it's like a, you know,

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.040
a reactor or a frontend thing.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.040
I want you to imagine that this is the image of your code base

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.520
that you hold in your head.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.040
Now, you might inside here have some vague groupings

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.280
of different functionality.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.760
For instance, here you might have, let's say,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.560
a thumbnail editor feature.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.480
And all of these different modules contribute to that.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.080
Over here you might have a little video editor feature

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:39.920
or something.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.040
Down here is all the code related to authentication.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.800
Up here is a bunch of crud forms for updating stuff,

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:45.880
maybe in a CMS.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.680
And over here are a couple of example features

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.640
that I can't be bothered to think of examples for.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.200
Now, this map that I've created here

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.440
of all of the located modules in this particular code base,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.360
they're not actually reflected that much in the file system.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.800
They're all really jumbled up together.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.040
If I want to just grab, let's say, an export from this module

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.640
and import it down into this module, I can,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:05.520
there's nothing stopping me.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.040
And so what you might end up with is a bunch

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.200
of kind of disparate relationships between stuff

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.040
that doesn't actually relate to each other.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.360
Now you as the developer understand the mental map

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:14.680
between all of these modules.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.600
But what the AI sees when it first goes into your code base

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.800
is this.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.200
It doesn't see all of the natural groupings

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.360
and all the natural relationships.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.880
What it sees is a bunch of disparate modules

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.280
that can all import from each other.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.280
That's because AI, when it jumps into your code base,

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.440
it has no memory, it has not experienced your code base

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.280
before.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.040
It's like the guy from Memento who just steps in

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.880
and goes, okay, I'm here, what am I doing?

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.720
So my first assertion here is that you need to make sure

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.360
that the file system and the design of your code base

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.400
matches this internal map that you have off it.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.400
This is because if you describe something

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.040
over in the video editor section

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.840
and you use it via a prompt,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.640
then you want the AI to be able to find it easily.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.560
The AI won't go in knowing every single function,

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.840
every single module, and what they're supposed to do

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.240
and how they link to each other.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:02.840
And the best way I have found to do that

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.720
is with deep modules.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.600
Now deep modules comes from this book here,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.240
which is a philosophy of software design.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.120
And the idea is that in order to make your system

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:12.960
easily navigable and easy to change

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.520
and also easy to test, is that you have a deep module

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.960
so lots of implementation control by a simple interface.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.640
Well that looks like in terms of our graph

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.600
is instead of many, many small modules,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.320
you end up with these big chunks of modules

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.680
with simple controllable interfaces.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.160
And this means that any exports from these modules

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.040
have to come from that interface.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.680
Now when I read that about deep modules,

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.760
I immediately thought about putting AI

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.640
in control of these modules

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.240
because this is an opportunity to introduce

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.320
a kind of seam into the code base.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.480
I don't really care about what's happening inside here,

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:48.840
which is the implementation.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.400
I just care about what's happening in the interface

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:55.000
because the interface, which is the publicly accessible API

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.560
of this module, I can carefully control

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.000
and I can apply my taste to and design.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.080
And then the stuff inside here,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.800
I can just delegate to an AI to control

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.120
and I can write tests that completely lock down

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:09.520
the module in terms of its behavior.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.120
So these are not just deep modules

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.520
with simple interfaces, they're also gray box modules.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.520
In other words, I don't actually need

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.840
to look inside these modules.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.320
I can if I want to, if I want to influence their outcome

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.960
or if I need to apply some taste to the implementation

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.120
or I need to improve their performance or something,

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.880
but as long as the tests are good,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.240
then I don't really need to care about what happens inside.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.760
Now this has three massive benefits.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.080
The first one is that I can make my code base

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.400
way more navigable.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.440
Let's, for the sake of argument,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.120
just call each of these services, right?

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.880
The video editor service, the thumbnail service, whatever.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.400
If I document these each inside their own folder

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.520
and I have the publicly accessible interface,

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.600
kind of like a really obvious inner type section,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.280
then the AI, when it's exploring my code base,

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.360
it can see all of these different services

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.240
on the file system.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.080
It can read and understand the types that they export

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.160
before it actually looks at the implementation.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.120
And then it can say, okay, I've seen the interface,

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.280
I understand what this does,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.480
I don't need to look inside

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.040
because I can just trust what it's returning.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:07.720
In other words, we've designed our code base

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.120
for progressive disclosure of complexity.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.400
The interface sits at the top

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.800
and it just explains what the module does.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.440
And then when we need to,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.520
we can look inside the module and make changes to it

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.960
or look at it to understand its behavior more deeply.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.160
The second one is that we reduce the cognitive burnout

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.520
of managing this code base.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.760
Now as a user, I can just go, right,

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.960
I need something from, I don't know, this made up feature

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.480
or let's say the authentication bit over here.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.040
Let's see what the public interface is.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.720
Let's just grab that and use it.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.080
And instead of needing to think about the interrelationships

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.080
between all of these modules,

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.400
I can just keep kind of like seven or eight lumps of stuff

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:47.520
in my head and go, okay, the AI manages the stuff inside that.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.600
I only need to worry about designing the interfaces

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:50.520
and how they fit together.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:52.440
Now this, of course, is still a million miles away

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.080
from vibe coding because you need to apply taste

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.920
at the boundaries of these modules.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.720
You need to be really good at deciding, okay,

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.080
what goes into that module, what goes into that module.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.520
And what you really want to avoid

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.760
are lots of little shallow modules,

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.360
which is kind of what we had up here, right?

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.200
Each of these modules is just like sure.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.760
It's kind of interrelated and grouped together,

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.440
but really there are lots of tiny shallow modules

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.720
which are testable in these tiny units,

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.280
which are really hard to keep all in your head.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:20.920
And so by simplifying the mental map of the code base,

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.800
we reduce cognitive burnout

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.480
that comes from managing this code base.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.280
And again, this is nothing new.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.240
This is a 20 year old software practice.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.240
And the third one here, I mean,

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.600
I'm really just repeating myself,

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.080
but this is what we've been doing all along.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:36.560
This is how good code bases have supposed to have been designed.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.000
So what works here for humans is also great for AI.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.440
We need to stop thinking about AI

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.160
as like this super powered developer

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.480
as like, you know, it's gonna reach AGI

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.680
and understand that it's got some weird limitations.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:48.920
And the limitations that it has

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.400
are that it's a new starter in your code base.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.920
So you need to make your code base

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.520
friendly and ready for new starters

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.960
because you're gonna be spawning like 20 new starters

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.920
every day here, probably more

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.800
just to look at your code base and make changes.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:04.520
So that means the map of your code base

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.040
needs to be easily navigable

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:09.120
and it needs to be enforced by using these modules.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.960
Now, some languages make this easier than others.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.480
For instance, in TypeScript and JavaScript,

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:16.600
it's actually not that easy to make these services

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:19.880
make these modules sort of boundary in this way.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:22.000
I want to give a quick shout out to Effect

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.720
because I posted a video on Effect a few months ago,

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.960
I'm actually using Effect way more than I did back then.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.400
And it makes this kind of sort of seeming

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.040
modularizing of your code base really simple.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:35.280
The final thing I want to say here

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.840
is that you need to be thinking about these modules

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:38.880
and how you're affecting them

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.840
and how you're designing the interfaces

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:43.000
in every coding session that you do.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.360
That means right from the early planning stage

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.000
when you're writing your PRDs

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.680
or when you're turning your PRDs into implementation issues,

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.120
you need to be thinking about the modules

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:52.960
that you're affecting and the interfaces

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.400
and how you are going to test them

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.640
because tests and feedback loops are essential for an AI

00:07:57.640 --> 00:07:59.560
because of course, they're essential

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:01.240
for a new starter joining the code base.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:03.640
If you want a new starter to contribute effectively,

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.160
you need a well-tested code base

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.240
so they can see what their changes do as they ripple out.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:09.400
So that's my rant for today.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.760
Your code base is probably not ready for AI

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.200
because you're not using enough deep modules.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.960
And instead you've got a web of interconnected

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.880
kind of shallow modules like this

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:20.800
which are really hard to navigate

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.000
and really hard to test and really hard to keep in your head.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.480
Now if you dig this, then of course you will dig my newsletter

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.480
where we go more deeply into topics like this.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:29.320
Thanks for watching folks.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:30.640
What else do you think goes into making

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:32.120
a great code base for AI?

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.560
I really love this metaphor for deep modules

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:36.160
but I know it's not the only one going.

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:37.440
There are plenty out there.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.360
Thanks for watching and I will see you very soon.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:41.360
So when you're thinking about your code base with AI,

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:42.680
what are you thinking about?

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:45.320
What kind of 20-year-old books do you want to recommend?

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:46.120
Leave it in the comments.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.200
It's the easiest way to keep up with all my stuff

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.680
and the link is below.

